when bring caching:

->it is introduced to scale the read
->expensive queries
->high database cpu
->low latency

how to introduce caching:


#caching->temp storage of data that keeps recently used data handy
         so get it much faster way next time.

#Accessing data
a:from database takes million secs as it store data in the disk.
b:from cache takes nano secs as it store data in the memory.

#where you have the cache
a:external caching like redis/memcached
    advantages:multiple services can read the cache data from external cache.
b:in process caching/shared same memory and cpu as the application
    advantage: reduce/eliminates the network call.
    trade-off:data inconsistency let say 1 service has the  cache other won't
                or cache update is not properly happens.

c:CDN(cache data across multiple geo-graphic location)
  mostly used for to store the static data such as media image/videos etc.
  advantage:optimise for network latency

d:client side caching: example rest apis
    advantage:avoid network call
    disadvantage:less control


#Cache Architectures

a:cache aside->check cache first if data is present return else get it from db
                store it in the cache and return the data to service.
                advantage:store only the relevant data which usr needs
                disadvantages:add latency if cache miss.

b:write through->write to the cache and sync write to db.
                 disadvantage:unnecessary data which is not req
                              ->cache full
                              ->dual write problem
                 advantage->no data loss
                            ->slower write

c:write behind/write back->similar to write through only diff is cache flush the data
                         in batches async to the db
                         dis:data loss in case cache crashed
                         adv:used when you req high throughput

d:read through:same as cache aside instead of application write the db in-case
               of cache miss cache read from db as fallback.


#cache eviction policy:
a:LRU->evict item that haven't been used recently ,most common .
b:LFU->evict item that are used least often
c:FIFO->evict the oldest item first.simple but rarely right choice
d:TTL->each item expires after a set time(eg 5min).great for the data that
       can go stale.

#cache challenges
1:cache stampede(thundering herd)->given a scenario if cache ttl is expired and
there is million/k of request suddenly comes to cache ,they all there is cache miss
so all req hit the db (becomes overwhelmed). and bottleneck

solution:
a:request coalescing->here only 1 request get access to reads from db and update cache.
b:cache warming->refresh the cache before ttl expires.

2:cache consistency:cache data is not consistent(profile upload)

there is no fixed solution but can answered
a:invalidate the write: when you are writing the database check the cache and remove the
    the old key from cache next time get the data from db and update the cache.
b:short ttl:to update the cache with fresh data from db.
c:if eventual consistency is fine then no problem.

3:Hot Key->a particular key asked requested multiple times becomes overwhelming
solution :
a:spread out that key to multiple shard/cache
b:client side caching